{{- /* Security headers for hosts that read a _headers file - Netlify and
       Cloudflare Pages. GitHub Pages ignores it, because it cannot serve
       custom response headers at all; there the meta CSP is the only policy
       that reaches the browser.

       Nothing is written unless the site declares the HEADERS output format -
       see "Security headers" in the README. */ -}}
# Sensible default security headers
#  - More info: https://scotthelme.co.uk/hardening-your-http-response-headers/
#  - Test website: https://securityheaders.com/
/*
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: sameorigin
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), camera=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()
{{- /* HSTS, with a safe default that a site can replace. One year and nothing
       else is right for any site: it protects the hosts this configuration
       serves and claims nothing about the rest of the zone.

       Adding includeSubDomains claims the whole zone, and preload puts that
       claim in a list shipped with the browser, which takes months to leave.
       Neither can be a default: on a site with one subdomain still on plain
       HTTP, includeSubDomains makes it unreachable with no way to click past
       the error. Set the parameter only after checking every name, including
       the ones nobody thinks of, and only if you accept it for every name the
       zone will ever have. */ -}}
{{- with site.Params.strictTransportSecurity }}
  Strict-Transport-Security: {{ . }}
{{- else }}
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000
{{- end }}
{{- /* Emitted only when the site set the policy in one place. A CSP here and
       a different one in the meta tag are not alternatives - a browser applies
       every policy it receives and enforces the intersection - so shipping a
       default here would quietly tighten any site that had customised its meta
       CSP, blocking whatever that policy allows and this one does not. */ -}}
{{- with site.Params.contentSecurityPolicy }}
  Content-Security-Policy: {{ . }}
{{- end }}
{{- /* The three isolation headers, opt-in for the same reason as the CSP above.
       None of them is a safe default.

       Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy severs the window reference between a page and
       whatever opened it, or whatever it opens. "same-origin" is right for a
       site that never talks to a popup, and wrong for one running an OAuth or
       payment window.

       Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy says who may load this site's files inside a
       page of their own. "same-origin" breaks any image, font or script another
       site legitimately embeds, a logo or a badge above all.

       Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy is the one that fails quietly. Under
       "require-corp" a cross-origin subresource that has not opted in simply
       does not load, with nothing shown to the visitor. Safe only on a site that
       loads no third-party resource at all, and it must be tested rather than
       assumed, because the day someone adds a map or a video it stops appearing
       and nobody learns why. */ -}}
{{- with site.Params.crossOriginOpenerPolicy }}
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: {{ . }}
{{- end }}
{{- with site.Params.crossOriginResourcePolicy }}
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: {{ . }}
{{- end }}
{{- with site.Params.crossOriginEmbedderPolicy }}
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: {{ . }}
{{- end }}
